  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/1/1d/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_47.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Ant-Man and the Wasp are finishing up capturing Ramond Theis, \"an internationally wanted criminal\" who was attempting a diamond heist, and afterward the Indian owner tells them of a man named Ghazandi who can hypnotize cobras and human with his music (but is careful not to, as playing the wrong notes doing that can completely sap his power).&nbsp; Jan convinces Hank to go see some jazz concert afterward, but they soon see a man named Trago, a petty crook and trumpet musician trying to make off with his manager's cash box.&nbsp; Ant-Man and the Wasp soon capture him.\n\nBut the manager decides not to press charges but instead gets him a ticket on the first plane out of the country, but tells him that's as far as friendship goes - \"Don't come back!\"&nbsp; He's being sent to Dehli, India.\n\nTwo months later, near New Dehli, India, we find Trago, who has of course tracked down Ghazandi - he's looking for the power he has to charm snakes with his music.&nbsp; He agrees to teach him.&nbsp; But Trago is impatient, wanting to hypnotize humans rather than just reptiles.&nbsp; Ghazandi warns of danger doing this.\n\n\"The months fly by...\" the text says as we go back to Ant-Man and the Wasp (<i>what have they been doing in the meantime</i>?).&nbsp; Trago is back in the US though - in Connecticut at a gig (as has the other musicians in his small combo under his spell too) - he uses his playing to mesmerize the crowd.&nbsp; He could take their money but decides not to - he's ready for New York, and to contract a local TV station to broadcast his music to millions.\n\nSoon, Wasp hears about \"Trago and his Magic Trumpet\" and calls Hank over to the radio.&nbsp; Soon, they're being mesmerized, but Hank calls out to Korr, a trusted ant who calls for help - the ants come and carry away the now-helpless pair.&nbsp; But the city is now hypnotized, and he commands them to find Ant-Man and also loot the banks.\n\nMeanwhile, Korr and his friends are carrying the two humans down into an ant-hill, out of the reach of the hypnotic blare of the trumpet.&nbsp; However, a garden snake strikes the ants, and Korr is done for before Ant-Man can save him.&nbsp; He gets on Foss, brother of the faithful Korr and heads off to the radio station to find Trago.&nbsp; However, Trago sees Hank and tries to smash him, but Hank jumps into the trumpet while his army of ants attacks Trago directly.&nbsp; Hank tampers with the trumpet, causing Trago to play the notes (\"by chance\" the text says - convenient) that sap his power.&nbsp; Trago wakes up, as if from a trance.&nbsp; So does the rest of the city.\n\nSince no theft had actually occurred yet, and since Trago has no memory of what he did, they're content to let him do what he loves best - play his trumpet for a living.&nbsp; Hank though is sad about his ant friend Korr.&nbsp; The story closes with the barf-worthy quote \"Greater love hath no one than this, that he lay down his life for his friends!\"&nbsp; (<i>Applying Scripture to an ant's actions</i>?&nbsp; <i>Come on</i>!)",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "<i>Ugh</i>, at first this seems like a middling tale, but it really descends into silliness.&nbsp; The fact that the Indian man at the beginning mentions Ghazandi and the notes that would sap powers so that Hank would know to attack the trumpet (\"months\" later to boot) is just too convenient to be forgiven.&nbsp; These people that show up in their lives and terrorize the city and then are let walk away scot free is becoming a ridiculous trend in this book and not very realistic.&nbsp; I find it annoying.&nbsp; And naming the ants too?&nbsp; <i>Argh</i> -- just poor.\n\nThroughout the story, Jan and Hank use phrases like \"boyfriend\" and \"Honey\" that clearly show that the relationship is now progressing, even though Hank also continues to call Jan \"kid.\"\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the end, the book was saved from a low score, <i>not</i> because the Ant-Man/Wasp story was any good, but because of the back-up story called \"Target... Earth!\"&nbsp; In fact, this story provided such a laugh-out-loud moment, that it immediately became a finalist for 1963's <b>Best Belly-Laugh Moment</b>.&nbsp; It was so funny that I am electing to skip the description here and save that for the Year-End Summary.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And since I have already mentioned that I have read through the entire 1960s at this point, I still cannot think of a single panel from any back-up story that is funnier than the last one in this short, five-page story!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>  In fact, it was so good that Stan re-used the ironic twist ending in one of his <i>Tales of the Watcher</i> features that was published almost a year later in <i>Tales of Suspense</i> 55.]",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}